1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,433
Zukai ships an MCP server.

2
00:00:03,433 --> 00:00:11,840
Which means the one drawing the diagram does not have to be you — it can be Claude.

3
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:28,551
Start the local dev server, point Claude at the zukai MCP server, and the diagram open in your browser becomes the workbench.

4
00:00:29,801 --> 00:00:33,717
Just ask for a three-tier AWS stack.

5
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:43,615
Claude reads the model, works out the nodes and the links, and sends them through zu_apply.

6
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:46,878
The canvas updates as it goes.

7
00:00:48,128 --> 00:00:50,551
Then: wrap them in subnet boxes.

8
00:00:50,551 --> 00:00:56,609
Claude reads the coordinates it already placed, and adds the frames behind them.

9
00:00:56,609 --> 00:00:59,637
The diagram grows with the conversation.

10
00:01:01,037 --> 00:01:03,602
What comes out is plain JSON.

11
00:01:03,602 --> 00:01:08,467
Keep drawing by hand, or commit it and review the diff.

12
00:01:08,467 --> 00:01:11,917
The AI and you edit the very same file.

13
00:01:13,217 --> 00:01:13,983
Zukai.

14
00:01:13,983 --> 00:01:17,813
Free, open source, no sign-up.

15
00:01:17,813 --> 00:01:21,133
The MCP setup is in USAGE.

16
00:01:21,133 --> 00:01:21,516
md.
